>> title: Nanbanzuke
>> category: Fried & Crispy
>> tags: japanese, fish, vinegar, fried, make ahead, summer
>> counters: Japanese Home Cooking
>> aka: 南蛮漬け, nanbanzuke, nanbanzuke, なんばんづけ, 鯵の南蛮漬け, aji no nanbanzuke, fried fish in sweet vinegar, Japanese escabeche
>> pairs-with: dashi, gohan, kinpira-gobo
>> servings: 4
>> time: 2 hr 40 min
>> slack: forgiving — the fish goes into the marinade hot; cold fish dropped into cold vinegar never takes it in and stays a separate crisp thing
>> step.1: Hot fish into cold vinegar, so the marinade is made first and left waiting.
>> step.2: warm 2 min, pour over the vegetables
>> step.3: dust and fry 4 min
>> step.4: slide in hot, then chill 2 hr

Hot fish into cool vinegar is the operation this dish is built on, and it has to happen while the fish is still spitting.

Warm @rice vinegar{1/2%cup}(120 mL), @dashi{1/2%cup}(120 mL), @soy sauce{3%Tbs}(45 mL), @mirin{2%Tbs}(30 mL) and @sugar{2%Tbs} in a #saucepan{} ~warm{2%min} until the sugar has gone, then pour it over @onion{1}(sliced paper-thin), @carrot{1/2}(in matchsticks) and @dried red chile{1}(seeded, in rings) laid in a shallow #dish{}.

Dust @horse mackerel{1%lb}(450 g; small whole fish, gutted and gilled, or small fillets) in @potato starch{1/4%cup}(30 g) and fry it in @neutral oil{2%cups}(475 mL; at 340°F / 170°C) ~fry{4%min}, until it is pale gold and the small bones have gone crisp enough to eat.

Slide @&(~1)fried fish{} straight into @&(2)nanbanzu{} while it is still hissing, turn each piece once so it is covered, and ~chill{2%hr} in the fridge before eating it.
